  <datafield tag="520" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">El muro is 9 by 14 inches with a semi-concealed wire-o binding opening to 20". The ten triptych photographs and text are printed on Shine, a luminous, mica-coated, acid-free paper. Three sheets of translucent magenta paper separate the photographs and the essays. Edition of 1200. Eduardo Hernández Santos's El muro is a composite chronicle of late-night life at Havana's utmost limit of the city: the seafront wall, popularly know as the Malecón. El muro is a significant visual source of constantly thwarted gay nightlife in Havana, but above all it is a social document about the members of a historical underclass emerging from hiding to contest efforts to control their lifestyles, to proclaim their normality and their non-conformity of the status quo. These powerful images express the subjects' abiding desire to reaffirm their existence.</subfield>
  </datafield>
